ed `romanDominationNumber G` is the minimum element of $S_R$. The `radius G` is the minimum eccentricity.\\n\\n\\n**Bounding roman domination number**\\n\\nDetermined that the `romanDominationNumber G` is less than or equal to the number of vertices, utilizing the properties of a constant function. Considered whether the `romanDominationNumber G` could be greater than or equal to the number of vertices, but disproved this using the properties of a constant Roman dominating function. Focused on finding the `radius G` definition.\\n\\n\\n**Refining inequality for Roman domination**\\n\\nDetermined the upper bound on eccentricity is the number of vertices, so radius is also bounded. Realized that while Roman domination number could be significantly less than the number of vertices, we are aiming to prove a lower bound for Roman domination using radius. Formulated the inequality $\\\\gamma_R(G) \\\\ge \\\\text{radius}(G) + 1$, which requires that the weight of any Roman dominating function must be at least radius(G) + 1. Initiated analysis of a generic Roman dominating function f, mapping vertices to {0,1,2}.\\n\\n\\n**Re-evaluating mathematical faithfulness**\\n\\nObserved the generality of the mathematical proof and the complexity of its formalization in Lean 4. Estimated the formalization could require significant lines of code. Questioned the feasibility of generating a flawless Lean 4 proof in one step.\\n\\nConsidering refuting `Candidate`. Mathematical validity is confirmed, but now suspect a potential defect within the Lean 4 formalization. Need to verify whether `Candidate` accurately represents the mathematical conjecture.\\n\\nReviewing notes about previous formalization bugs that produced apparent successes. The notes highlight potential vulnerabilities in the formalization, and their discovery through axiom audit.\\n\\n\\n**Investigating the `Candidate` formalization**\\n\\nAnalyzed the `Candidate` definition to understand its components and potential formalization issues. Focused on unpacking `HasRadius` and its relation to `ballWithin` and graph connectivity. Determined that `HasRadius` implies graph connectivity due to `ballWithin` reaching the entire vertex set at step $k = \\\\text{card } V$.\\n\\nInspected the definition of `eccentricity`, which uses `minUpTo`, to understand how the radius is defined. `minUpTo n p` filters `range (n + 1)` based on the predicate `p`.\\n\\n\\n**Examining definitions' types and domains**\\n\\nDetermined `eccentricity G v` calculates the minimum $k$ such that a ball of radius $k$ centered at $v$ covers the entire graph. Examined `radius G` and found that it is computed as the minimum of eccentricities of all vertices in $G$. Also, analyzed `romanDominationNumber G` definition, in particular understanding the role of `univ`.\\n\\nConfirmed `univ` in the context of `V → Fin 3` represents the set of all functions from vertices $V$ to `Fin 3`. Recognized that since $V$ and `Fin 3` have `Fintype`, the set of all such functions is finite, with size $3^{|V|}$.\\n\\n\\n\",\"tool_calls\":[{\"name\":\"view_file\",\"args\":{\"AbsolutePath\":\"\\\"/mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/benchmark/challenges_promising13/Challenge_8ca1b27c.lean\\\"\",\"EndLine\":\"25\",\"StartLine\":\"1\",\"toolAction\":\"\\\"View Challenge_8ca1b27c.lean\\\"\",\"toolSummary\":\"\\\"View 8ca1b27c\\\"\"}}]}\n206: {\"step_index\":210,\"source\":\"MODEL\",\"type\":\"GENERIC\",\"status\":\"DONE\",\"created_at\":\"2026-09-11T15:02:23Z\",\"content\":\"Created At: 2026-09-11T17:02:23+02:00\\nCompleted At: 2026-09-11T17:02:23+02:00\\nFile Path: `file:///mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/benchmark/challenges_promising13/Challenge_8ca1b27c.lean`\\nTotal Lines: 20\\nTotal Bytes: 589\\nShowing lines 1 to 20\\nThe following code has been modified to include a line number before every line, in the format: <line_number>: <original_line>. Please note that any changes targeting the original code should remove the 